meta {
  name: POST Sync
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseURL}}/api/v1/devices/sync/
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "data_source": "{{rook_data_source}}"
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
}

docs {
  The returned connection includes syncProgress operation metadata. A 202 response means accepted/coalesced, not completed. Refresh GET Providers with backoff for saved connection status. A completed_no_new_data outcome does not change latestMeasurementAt. Existing six-hour cooldown and response envelope are preserved.
  Manual Sync uses the existing endpoint and body. HTTP 202 means a request was accepted; data includes jobId, alreadyQueued, syncRequestAccepted=true, cooldownActive=false and nextSyncAllowedAt. A running job is reused with a newer target retained for a follow-up window; its live plan is never overwritten.

  Within six elapsed hours of the last accepted manual request for this connection, HTTP 200 returns queued=false, cooldownActive=true, syncRequestAccepted=false, jobId (when active) and nextSyncAllowedAt. This is an informational no-op, not a second job. Initial history, webhooks and automatic retries do not spend the manual cooldown.

  Inspect the returned connection for importStatus, lastManualSyncQueuedAt, lastSyncRequestedAt, lastPullCompletedAt, syncedThrough and pendingSyncRequestedAt. Accepted is not completed. Run a second request immediately to verify cooldown; boundary tests use a controlled staging clock, not repeated production requests.

  Dedicated ROOK health/recovery ECS consumers and reviewed migrations are required. The old 15-minute recovery schedule must be disabled during cutover. All auth is inherited; use environment values only.
}
